#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184098 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184201 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f0567d5-4622-5c34-a448-0bc043fec12f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Function Given Graphically"}] :question/id #uuid "9bdbb969-4745-503a-ba4b-fb07529b1b6c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9627" :question/problem "![](images/57/57be4d7696363594e9d100ba75a2df1acc9742b8604ead7c9d1176c51571032d.png)\n\nThe function $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Calculate $f(-2) + f(1)$." :question/worked-solution "We can find the necessary function values using the graph.\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- When $x=-2$, we have $y=-2$. So, $f(-2)=-2$.\n- When $x=1$, the function is **undefined** because the function has an open circle at this point.\n\nTherefore, since $f(1)$ is undefined, the sum $f(-2)+f(1)$ is undefined too."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184099 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184202 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55343d43-c173-5ba8-9a0e-58a76a282b7f" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "3ab53f11-138b-5c41-ae90-0158c3fc8a5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9636" :question/problem "What is the graph of $y = f(x)$, where $f(x) = 2x - 3$?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the graph of $y = 2x - 3$ is a straight line, and two distinct points can determine any straight line.\n\nSo, given any two distinct values of $x$, we can find the corresponding $y$-values by substituting $x$ into the formula $y = 2x - 3$.\nFor example,\n\n- if $x = 0$ then $y = 2(0) - 3 = -3$, which gives the point $(0,-3)$, and\n- if $x = 1$ then $y = 2(1) - 3 = -1$, which gives the point $(1,-1)$.\n\nFinally, we plot these $(x,y)$ pairs in the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This gives the following graph.\n\n![](images/9f/9f3005c9fac13668b126396f11aa8f3141d6de63d6e88e90d270901a9253fbd1.png)"}]}
